-- title:
--   A henselian tame descent base containing π with π^{q^2-1}=q
-- statement:
--   Let $q$ be a prime and let $A$ be a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb Q} =$ `AlgebraicClosure ℚ` lying over $q$, in the sense that the image of $q$ in $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ belongs to `A.nonunits`, the set of non-units of $A$; let $\pi \in \overline{\mathbb Q}$ satisfy $\pi^{q^2-1} = q$ and $\pi \in A$. The assertion is the existence of a subfield $K_0 \subseteq \overline{\mathbb Q}$ such that $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ is algebraic over $K_0$, together with a type $A_0$ carrying the structure of a commutative ring which is a domain, a discrete valuation ring and a henselian local ring, a ring homomorphism $\iota : A_0 \to A$ which is a local homomorphism, and an element $\varpi_0 \in A_0$, subject to: $\pi \in K_0$; $\iota$ is injective; the image in $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ of $\iota(A_0)$ is exactly the intersection of $A$ with $K_0$ as subsets of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$; the composite of $\iota$ with the residue map of $A$ onto its residue field is surjective; the maximal ideal of $A_0$ is the principal ideal generated by $\varpi_0$; and the image of $\varpi_0$ in $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ is $\pi$.
--
--   This provides the tame descent base for the valuation ring $A$: a subfield $K_0$ of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, obtained classically as the inertia field of $A$ over $\mathbb Q$ adjoined $\pi$, whose trace $A \cap K_0$ is a henselian discrete valuation ring with uniformiser $\pi$ and with residue field all of that of $A$. It is the base over which the semistable model of the full-level-$q$ modular curve is descended, and is invoked by the statements asserting existence of such a semistable scheme descent along valuation subrings.
